FOUR SPELLINGS OF ONE CHARACTER
   "\xe9"                                   -> 'é'  U+00E9   a BYTE value, extended to a code point
   "\u00e9"                                 -> 'é'  U+00E9   a 16-bit code unit
   "\U000000e9"                             -> 'é'  U+00E9   a full 32-bit scalar value
   "\N{LATIN SMALL LETTER E WITH ACUTE}"    -> 'é'  U+00E9   a NAME, looked up in the table
   all four equal? True

NOW U+1F600, WHICH IS ABOVE U+FFFF
   "\U0001F600"  -> '😀'  works: \U takes a scalar value
   "\u1F600"      ->  NOT that character: \u reads exactly four
                     digits, so it is U+1F60 then the digit 0 -> 'ὠ0'

That four-digit limit is the whole lesson. \u is a UTF-16 CODE UNIT
escape, and a code unit is 16 bits, so it cannot name anything above
U+FFFF on its own. Java and JSON have only that form, which is why a
JSON emoji is written as a SURROGATE PAIR:
   \uD83D\uDE00   -- two escapes, one character, and neither half is a character

So the escape a language gives you is a statement about what it thinks
a character IS:
   Rust   \u{1F600}   a scalar value, any width, braces so it can be
   Python \U0001F600  a scalar value, fixed 8 digits -- plus \N{...},
                      the only form that names rather than numbers
   Java   \uD83D\uDE00  two UTF-16 code units, because that is its str
   C      none of the above -- \x is bytes, and what those bytes MEAN
          is the compiler's execution charset, not a string feature
